// Sample family: every person and record cited here is fictional.
// This section covers Joan's parents too, so the Treloar line has its own account.

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Joan Margaret Treloar grew up in Mineral Point, the daughter of Thomas Henry Treloar and his wife, Mary. A typed list of Treloar names, found among Joan's papers, gives Thomas's birth at Mineral Point on 9 August 1898 and Joan's on 19 January 1925.#src("thomas-birth-list")#src("joan-birth-list") Nobody knows who typed the list or where its dates came from, so it is a starting point, not a settled account.
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
In 1930 Thomas was renting a house in the city and working as a laborer at the zinc works.#src("thomas-census-1930")#src("thomas-zinc-works-1930") His son Frank was seven that year, and Joan was five.#src("frank-treloar::frank-census-1930")#src("joan-census-1930")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Mary's own family is still unknown. Her maiden name was never passed down, and the typed list calls her only "Mary, his wife."#src("mary-treloar-list") Joan remembered her baking heavy cake every Saturday and, many years later, wrote the recipe down for her own children.#src("mary-treloar-heavy-cake")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
#refp("robert-john-penhallow") grew up on his parents' farm near Dodgeville. He enlisted in the U.S. Army at Madison on 15 October 1942, giving his work as farm hand.#src("robert-enlistment-1942") He served in England and France and came home in 1945.#src("robert-service-family") While he was away, Joan kept in touch with his family. In the spring of 1944 she brought his father a pasty and asked after him.#src("joan-letter-1944")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Robert and Joan married at Mineral Point on 14 June 1947.#src("penhallow-treloar-marriage-1947") They made their home in Madison, where Robert worked as a machinist until he retired.#src("robert-machinist") Their daughter Carol was born in 1950 and their son #refp("david-penhallow") in 1958.#src("carol-penhallow::carol-birth")#src("david-penhallow::david-birth")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
For Christmas 1995 Joan typed the family's favourite recipes into a small booklet, _Joan's Kitchen_.#src("joan-recipe-booklet-1995") It holds her mother-in-law Edith's pasties, along with saffron buns and her own mother's heavy cake. It also has lefse and apple kuchen from the family of David's wife, Karin.#src("joan-recipe-booklet-1995")
]

